  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 142 PM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nThis afternoon through tonight...\nFairly quiet conditions are expected through the near term period\nunder high pressure conditions and light winds. Skies will remain\nfairly clear but will have some high clouds move through during\nthe evening to overnight. As winds drop off overnight, some patchy\nground fog may be possible again at times, but expect impacts to\nbe minimal.\n\nActive Weather Tuesday into Wednesday...\nA fast moving shortwave will drop through the region Tuesday \nevening followed shortly by a cold front overnight, bringing with\nthem active weather through the first half of Wednesday. Ahead of\nthese features, daytime heating ahead could still produce a few\nshowers, but would expect the focus to be later in the day so \nkept a 20-40% chance early. As the shortwave drops through in the\nevening, brought some higher chances (50-70%) with the focus being\nalong the Upper Peninsula border further north. This period will\nbe the one to watch for any stronger storm potential as surface\ninstability and shear should suffice for organized convection,\nwith large hail and damaging winds as the main threats. The \nbetter parameters all align to our northwest, so any stronger\nstorms should form upstream before slowly working their way\neastwards. As of this forecast issuance, the Marginal Risk area \nexpands into western Vilas and Oneida counties so storm will still\nneed watching to see if they hold together. If the low level jet\nand surface instability prove more robust in upcoming forecasts,\ncould see the risk area expand another county or two eastwards \nfor the evening period. The cold front then arrives overnight, \nbringing more widespread rainfall across the whole area (60-80% \nchance). The overnight timing will not favor any stronger storms, \nwith any rain and thunder likely remaining elevated. Although \nsevere/strong storms aren't expected, some thunder could still mix\nin at times. Probability of exceeding half an inch of rainfall \nremains around 50%, mainly for northern Wisconsin, but would still\nexpect a few localized areas to get closer to an inch of rain \nunder the stronger convection. The cold front will still be \npushing across the region Wednesday morning, so some precipitation\nwill still be in place across eastern Wisconsin, but areas \nfurther west will likely already see dry conditions. \n\nRest of the Forecast...\nAnother extended period of dry conditions is expected for the \nregion for the rest of the work week. No active weather is \nexpected until the broad ridge over southwestern CONUS shifts \neastwards enough to bring a few shortwaves across the northern \nedge into our area. Current forecast doesn't bring the first of\nthese through the early until early Saturday. Early look at severe\npotential keeps the stronger weather to our south, but this will\nneed monitoring as well as we get closer to the weekend.\n\nTemperatures will change little over the work week, with highs in\nthe upper 70s to lower 80s during the afternoon, and lows in the\nmiddle to upper 50s (a few lower 60s overnight). The Midwest time\nof year you may want a light sweater in the morning and wish you\ndidn't have it with you by the afternoon.",
